00:01:08.399 --> 00:01:13.530
I will start with explaining what is pipelining
in HTTP/1.

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:19.759
So normally, when the browser sends a request,
or when you send a request from Burp, it looks

00:01:19.759 --> 00:01:22.700
like this: one requests, one response.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:24.100
Nothing unusual.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:29.990
However, you can also send two requests at
the same time and the server will give you

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:31.110
two responses.

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:38.310
Although, this is not used by browsers anymore,
it's a feature, not a bug, at least not by itself.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:45.880
But how does the server differentiate between
this being two separate requests or one with

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.139
a body?

00:01:48.139 --> 00:01:52.880
To help the server solve this problem, we
use the Content-Length header.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.889
It tells the server, in bytes, how long our request
is.

00:01:57.889 --> 00:02:04.780
And the server knows that, whenever the first
request is finished, the next one begins.

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:09.640
If you are using Burp's repeater, this header
is updated automatically.

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:15.439
If you try to send these two requests with
default settings, Burp will update the Content-Length

00:02:15.439 --> 00:02:20.110
header and you will actually send one big
request.

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:26.099
However, if you turn that option off and set
the header to zero, the server will see two

00:02:26.099 --> 00:02:28.480
requests and issue two responses.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:34.000
Of course, if HTTP pipelining is supported.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.550
But not every single request contains the
Content-Length header.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:43.959
In fact, most of them don't because most of
requests use the get method which doesn't

00:02:43.959 --> 00:02:45.450
contain a body.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:49.239
So this header is useless because it would
always be set to zero.

00:02:49.239 --> 00:02:55.329
And the server just doesn't expect a body
when you are requesting, let's say, a static

00:02:55.329 --> 00:02:56.769
image.

00:02:56.769 --> 00:03:02.829
If you manually change the method from get
to, for example, post, the servers still respond

00:03:02.829 --> 00:03:08.069
with this static image which doesn't really
make sense to me, but it's probably to be

00:03:08.069 --> 00:03:10.909
even more error-prone.

00:03:10.909 --> 00:03:16.980
The fact that normally these requests don't
have a body doesn't mean you can't send one.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.530
Let's see what happens when you do.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:24.340
This is a simple image request which we can
send to a vulnerable server.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:31.879
We can do the same using post method. And also,
we can use the request body, but the server

00:03:31.879 --> 00:03:35.360
doesn't use it in any way.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:41.910
And let's prepare the body of the request
so that it looks like another HTTP request.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:47.810
If we send this, do you think we will get
one or two responses?

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:54.019
Based on what I said earlier, this is one
big request with a body of 30 bytes.

00:03:54.019 --> 00:03:57.709
So we expect to get one response.

00:03:57.709 --> 00:04:02.180
But this is when we get to the client-side
desync problem.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:08.989
Because even though we sent the valid Content-Length
header with a value of 30, the vulnerable

00:04:08.989 --> 00:04:12.110
server did not use this header.

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:18.860
It's because this endpoint never consumes
a request body, so it assumes the Content-Length

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:20.340
header is zero.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:27.230
And it probably worked fine for years without
any problem, before James Kettle came with his new

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:29.330
research.

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:35.420
So what a vulnerable server sees is that the
first request ends here and this is a second

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:40.880
one, meaning it will also send two responses.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.980
The question you should be asking yourself
now is why is that actually a problem?

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:49.750
Because this is not really different from
this.

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:54.940
And we could basically send the second version
anyway.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:56.860
And that's a very good question.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:03.040
But the breakthrough with client-side desync
is that with previous requests smuggling techniques,

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:09.070
you had to use Burp or even tools that worked
on the lower network level.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:16.430
But this attack can work with the browser
because when we send this request, there is

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:20.070
absolutely nothing out of line.

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:26.600
It just happens to have the body that looks
like an HTTP request, but it's nothing invalid.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:31.810
It's the server that ignores the Content-Length
header and assumes it to be zero.

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:38.800
You can use this to confuse the browser because
the browser wants the amount of requests to

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:42.110
be equal to the amount of responses.

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:49.430
And in this case, the browser thinks it sent
one request, but it got back two responses.

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:55.230
So what does it do? When it sees the next request
using the same connection,

00:05:55.230 --> 00:06:05.270
it will respond to it with the unexpected
second response.

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:10.000
There's a whole variety of ways you can use
to exploit this bug.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:17.517
But in this case, let's see the technique
that allowed to hack many websites built on Akamai with an XSS.

00:06:18.434 --> 00:06:24.782
The first thing you need for this technique
to work is an endpoint that ignores the Content-Length header.

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:31.260
Your best candidates are static files, redirects
or different errors.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:36.760
The bug was in Akamai itself, so it affected
many websites, but we'll stick to an example

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:40.000
of the exploit on Capital One.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:45.450
If you requested a file without the trailing
slash, it redirected you to the same location

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:47.930
but with the trailing slash.

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:52.030
You could also use a post method for this.

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:57.040
And this is what you sent to the server to
confirm that it ignores the Content-Length

00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:58.110
header.

00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:01.250
There are actually three requests.

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:05.940
The first is the one that you want to check
if it's vulnerable to client side desync.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:11.440
With the second and the third one, you must
be able to differentiate them by looking at

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:12.470
the response.

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:18.620
Because you will get some response from the
server and you must determine which request

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:21.840
did the server respond to.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:28.430
In this case, let's say for the robots.txt
request, we expect 200 OK response code and

00:07:28.430 --> 00:07:32.120
for the /assets/ we expect 403 Forbidden.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:38.710
The value of the Content-Length header of
the first request must be between the second

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:40.550
and the third request.

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:46.090
If you count the characters, there are 24
in the first line, 4 in the second line, and

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:50.890
the new line is actually two bytes, one for
the carriage return and one for the actual

00:07:50.890 --> 00:07:54.220
new line, which gives us 30.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:57.170
When you send this, you should get two responses.

00:07:57.170 --> 00:08:03.950
If everything works fine, the server will
see the first request with this one as a body,

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:06.860
which ends exactly between requests.

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:10.550
So this would be our second request.

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:16.120
So for the first one you expect a redirect,
and for the second one you expect the response

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:20.310
to the /assets/ request, which means you expect
the 403.

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:25.460
If that's is the case, it means you did not
find client side desync vulnerability.

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:32.590
However, if the server assumes the Content-Length
of the first request to be zero, you will

00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:34.820
also get two responses.

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:41.270
But the second one will be the response to
the robots.txt request.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:46.430
The server will also see a strange looking
header, but it's nothing out of line.

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:53.130
If that's the case, you are very likely that
you just found a browser powered desync.

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:57.110
To confirm this, you can use this perfectly
valid fetch call.

00:08:57.110 --> 00:09:03.710
In fact, if you prefer, you can simply start
testing without using Burp but with this fetch

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:05.810
call.

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:13.370
What will happen under the hood is the browser
will send this, thinking it's a one big HTTP request.

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:16.120
The server, however, will ignore the Content-Length
header.

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:23.410
It will send two responses over the same connection.
The second one will be left hanging.

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:28.710
So the browser will match the first response
to the first request, as it should, and will

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:30.300
want to follow the redirect.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:34.660
So it will send another request over the same
connection.

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:38.270
And in this connection, there are already
two responses.

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:44.370
So the browser will simply return the second
response to the request.

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:51.670
Let's see how we could turn this client-side
desync into an XSS on Akamai websites.

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:57.640
Like every client side bug, client-side desync
thing is executed from the victim's browser,

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:03.290
which means that we need to prepare our malicious
website with malicious JavaScript code and

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:07.480
trick the victim into visiting our website.

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:11.930
This is the setup that we want to achieve
in our connection.

00:10:11.930 --> 00:10:15.980
The first request is our initial, vulnerable
one.

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:22.570
The second one is a request to which the server
will respond with text/html, Content-Type

00:10:22.570 --> 00:10:28.430
and this response must have a Content-Length,
header, even though it's a response to a head

00:10:28.430 --> 00:10:32.170
request so it won't have a body.

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:38.070
The third request will also contain an XSS
payload in a path, which will be reflected

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:39.630
in the response header.

00:10:39.630 --> 00:10:44.210
Normally, this is not the problem and it's
not exploitable, so it shouldn't be that hard

00:10:44.210 --> 00:10:46.360
to find.

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:52.380
And at this point, the browser will think
that it only sent one request while there

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:55.340
are three responses in the same connection.

00:10:55.340 --> 00:11:03.020
So the fourth request sent by the browser will
be poisoned by headers from the second response

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:13.430
and headers from the third one will be the
body of the response.

00:11:13.430 --> 00:11:18.840
So we want to make sure that this fourth request
will not be just a fetch call, but it will

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:25.400
be a top-level navigation so that the XSS
payload executes in the victim's browser.

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:32.190
But normally, the first request will return
a 301 so the fourth request would be the browser

00:11:32.190 --> 00:11:35.310
following this redirect as a fetch call.

00:11:35.310 --> 00:11:40.421
To prevent this, we use mode:'cors' which
will cause the browser to throw an error and

00:11:40.421 --> 00:11:42.670
not follow this redirect.

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:48.370
And instead, in the catch block, will use
the top-level navigation to redirect the

00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:54.260
victim to a new website which will be poisoned
with our headers and our body.

00:11:54.260 --> 00:11:59.710
And the last problem is that if these two
responses are left hanging for too long, the

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:02.250
browser will just discard them.

00:12:02.250 --> 00:12:07.210
So we add the cache buster parameter to the
head request so that it's processed a little

00:12:07.210 --> 00:12:10.750
bit longer to prevent this.

00:12:10.750 --> 00:12:19.980
So this is the final exploit that, if sent
from victim's browser, would result in an XSS

00:12:23.089 --> 00:12:29.210
If my video helped you to understand client-side
desync, I'm very happy and I'm proud of you

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:31.680
because I know this is not easy.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:37.240
However, this video did not cover the whole
research and I will write about some other

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:40.710
parts of it in next week's BBRE Premium.

